We have this blank folder here that I just git initted. It's called Epochup.
We are going to create an application that is fast to convert dates to and from unix epoch.
The application should be a GUI application written in Rust, and it should have an input field where you can enter some text. If most of the text entered is an epoch value or an epoch ms value, or an epoch float, or epoch ms float, then we should immediately get shown the datetime underneath to the bottom left. There should also be alternatives next to it (a different color maybe?) that is selectable to be copied to clipboard. Those formats should be other common datetime formats that are different than our local `LC_TIME`.
For the reverse case that the user enters a date in the text field, it should be *even more* flexible. Test all possible date formats, be smart about it. Even in huge blobs of text it should try to find some date in there, and show the epoch underneath to the left, with the epoch ms as an alternative. If there are multiple dates found, then it should only pick the first one.
Emacs org mode with C-C C-S is one example of something that is very flexible to understand what date you're trying to enter. You enter "Fri", then it will match the next possible Friday, revealing the epoch underneath.
The application is meant to be launched as a popup. It should be started by pressing some shortcut on sway (that we will do all the way in the end), and it should already look into the clipboard and do the same mechanism as mentioned above, but with the text field looking like a placeholder text, and the same outputs underneath still.
Pressing enter should copy the value at the bottom left to the clipboard.
In general, the application is meant to only serve 1 purpose, fast, but with an overflowing amount of ways to do that single 1 thing. To convert between date and epoch.
